@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+# iceland-supervooc
+
+80 W SUPERVOOC charging for the OnePlus Pad 4 (`iceland`, Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5) on mainline Linux.
+
+The module speaks the VOOC protocol to the stock brick and runs the tablet's two SC8547D charge pumps. It asks the brick for the current the stock firmware asks for at each state of charge, and it stops on the stock firmware's limits or tighter ones.
+
+> [!WARNING]
+> This driver puts up to 14 A into a lithium battery, and one tablet with one brick has run it. Sit next to the tablet for your first sessions. Read [Not tested](#not-tested) before you load the module.
+
+## Status
+
+Plug the brick in and a session starts. I measured these on one Pad 4 with the stock 80 W brick:
+
+| Asked from the brick | Into the battery | Pump temperature |
+|---|---|---|
+| 7.3 A | 14.0 to 14.2 A, 55 W | 64 to 70 °C |
+| 5.0 A | 9.4 to 9.7 A | 51 °C |
+| 3.8 A | 7.1 A | |
+| 3.0 A | 5.5 A, 25 W | 36 to 38 °C |
+
+An automatic session from 36 % asked for 7.3 A, then 5.0 A, then 3.8 A. It reached 75 % after 41 minutes and ended at 84 % after 58 minutes, on the last step of the taper. From there the firmware's charger finishes the charge at about 1.5 A.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- A kernel built from the [iceland-linux](https://github.com/mzakamin-hub/iceland-linux) patch queue plus the two patches in `patches/`. The first enables the i2c buses of the two pumps. The second lets a driver hold the firmware's charger off through `qcom_battmgr` for the length of a session. I ran 7.4.0-rc1 with the queue at `2fa5838`.
+- The stock 80 W brick and its cable. The driver accepts adapter ids `0x4b` to `0x4e` and `0x65`; I have run `0x4b`. It leaves other chargers to the firmware.
+- Wi-Fi power save off: `iw dev wlan0 set power_save off`. The driver ends a session after two replies slower than 1.5 ms. With ath12k's power save on, two replies in two short runs took 1.8 and 1.95 ms. With it off, the slowest reply across the sessions took 0.9 ms.
+
+## Build
+
+```sh
+make -C /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build M=$PWD modules
+```
+
+On NixOS:
+
+```nix
+boot.extraModulePackages = [ (config.boot.kernelPackages.callPackage ./iceland-supervooc { }) ];
+boot.kernelModules = [ "iceland_supervooc" ];
+boot.extraModprobeConfig = "options iceland_supervooc stage=4 autostart=1";
+networking.networkmanager.wifi.powersave = false;
+```
+
+## Use
+
+```sh
+modprobe iceland_supervooc stage=4 autostart=1
+```
+
+A bare `modprobe` charges nothing: the default `stage=2` runs the handshake and leaves the pumps off.
+
+| Parameter | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `stage` | 2 | 2 = handshake, 3 = one capped run you start by hand, 4 = automatic |
+| `autostart` | 0 | stage 4: one session per plug-in |
+| `cap_100ma` | 73 | highest bus current the driver asks for, in 100 mA |
+| `hold_s` | 4500 | longest session, in seconds |
+| `soc_min`, `soc_max` | 0, 80 | start window, % |
+| `temp_min`, `temp_max` | 210, 350 | start window, battery temperature in 0.1 °C |
+| `conn_max_c` | 60 | connector temperature limit, °C |
+| `auth_gate` | 0 | 1 = refuse a battery that fails the firmware's authentication |
+
+The defaults are those of stage 4. `modinfo` lists the stage 3 values and the timing parameters. You set parameters at load; a combination outside the ranges stops the load.
+
+The kernel log gets the start, each change of the current and the end reason. Load with `dyndbg=+p` for the readings of both pumps at 10-second intervals.
+
+`/sys/kernel/debug/iceland_supervooc/` holds:
+
+- `summary`: the last session, from its end reason to its reply times
+- `start` and `stop`: write 1 to start a session by hand or to end the running one
+- `release`: write 1 to end a charger hold that outlived its session
+- `regs`: a register dump of the pumps
+
+## A session
+
+1. **Gate.** The driver starts with a DCP charger, a battery between 21 and 35 °C, at most 80 %, 3.45 to 4.45 V, and a connector below 60 °C. It reads a failed gate again each minute while the brick stays plugged in, so a battery at 0 % starts once normal charging has lifted it to 3.45 V.
+2. **Handshake.** D+/D- go to the VOOC PHY in the primary pump. The adapter identifies itself and raises VBUS to twice the battery voltage, and the primary pump starts. The secondary pump joins above 1.4 A.
+3. **Current.** The driver asks for 7.3 A below 40 %, 5.0 A below 55 % and 3.8 A from there. The stock firmware runs these currents at those states of charge.
+4. **Heat.** The driver caps the ask at 6.0 A once a pump reaches 66 °C.
+5. **Taper.** After three gauge reads at 4.45 V the driver lowers the ask one step: 6.0, 5.0, 3.8, 3.0, 2.5, 2.0 A.
+6. **End.** The session ends at 90 %, above 4.48 V, at 4.45 V on the 2.0 A step, or after 75 minutes. The driver switches the pumps off first, runs the stock exit sequence, and hands D+/D- and the charger back to the firmware.
+
+You get one session per plug-in. The adapter asks for a current once, so the driver sends a lower ask the way stock does: it answers a battery-voltage frame with code `0x7f` and the adapter asks again.
+
+## Limits
+
+Each of these ends the session, pumps first. The ids name the limits in the tests.
+
+| Id | Limit |
+|---|---|
+| A1 | pumps on for longer than `hold_s` |
+| A3 | a pump die above 70 °C |
+| A5 | bus current above the ask + 300 mA on 3 reads |
+| A6 | battery above 4.48 V |
+| L1 | a fault flag from a pump |
+| L2, L3 | battery current above 16 A or below -3 A for more than 7 seconds |
+| L4 | one pump above 5.3 A |
+| | VBUS more than 500 mV + 0.3 mV per mA over 1 A above twice the battery voltage, on 3 reads |
+| | battery outside 0 to 44 °C, below 3.45 V, or its readings older than 3 seconds |
+| | connector at `conn_max_c` on 3 reads |
+| | no frame from the adapter for 200 ms, or two replies slower than 1.5 ms |
+| | a protection register that differs from the image the driver wrote |
+
+The driver sends a pump register write to the bus only if the table for the current phase of the session lists that register and value.
+
+## Tests
+
+```sh
+make -C tests test        # cc and python3, no kernel tree
+make -C tests mutations   # about 10 minutes
+```
+
+`vooc_logic.h` holds the protocol, the write tables and the limits, and the module and the tests compile the same file. `make test` replays an i2c trace of the stock firmware against it and crosses each limit from both sides: 22 million checks. `make mutations` breaks the logic and the driver in 398 ways, one at a time, and fails if the tests pass on a broken copy.
+
+## Not tested
+
+- this revision under power: the sessions above ran the same protocol and limit code, before I renamed the module and its parameters and moved the session summary out of the kernel log. This revision passes the host tests and loads on the tablet with the start window shut.
+- a second tablet, or a brick other than the stock 80 W one
+- a replug: the automatic session ran from a brick that sat in the port at boot
+- the 66 °C step: the hottest automatic session peaked at 65.0 °C
+- a start with the battery below 3.45 V
+- suspend during a session: the driver holds a wakeup source, and I have not put the tablet to sleep in the middle of one
+
+## Credits
+
+- DocZ13 wrote `sc8547d_cp.c` and the identification test in [iceland-linux issue #2](https://github.com/mzakamin-hub/iceland-linux/issues/2). The register defines, the ADC scale and the idea of a write whitelist come from there.
+- The handshake and the frame engine follow `oplus_ap_voocphy.c` in OnePlus's GPL charger source, [android_kernel_modules_and_devicetree_oneplus_sm8850](https://github.com/OnePlusOSS/android_kernel_modules_and_devicetree_oneplus_sm8850) at `07ca84e`.
+- mzakamin-hub maintains the iceland-linux patch queue this builds on.
